IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF MEASURE “H”
Background:
In 2005, Indian Wells voters approved a City ballot measure, which is commonly
referred to as “Measure H”. Measure H enacted a City general admissions tax
at a maximum rate of 10 percent (10%) on the admission charge to an event,
as deﬁned in Measure H. Measure H currently includes tax exemptions for the
following:
▪ Admission charges for events by bona ﬁde non-proﬁt organizations such
as country clubs, social clubs, tennis clubs, governmental entities and
schools; and
▪ Admission charges paid by “event sponsors”, as deﬁned in Measure H.
This Measure:
On July 2, 2026, the City Council placed Measure “H” on the November 3, 2026,
General Municipal Election ballot. The purpose of Measure “H” is to update the
Municipal Code by amending and clarifying the types of admissions charges
exempted from the City’s admissions tax. Measure “H” provides that admissions
charges for events conducted by the following entities shall be tax exempt:
▪ Organizations organized and operated exclusively for charitable, religious,
scientiﬁc, literary, or educational purposes and that have been determined
by the United States Internal Revenue Service to be exempt from federal
taxation under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code;
▪ Federal, state, and local governmental agencies, and membership
organizations whose members are federal, state, or local governmental
agencies, such as the League of California Cities, California State
Association of Counties; and
▪ Political organizations that are exempt from federal taxation under Section
527 of the Internal Revenue Code.
Measure “H” further provides that admissions charges for events held at a legally
established and operating hotel in the City will be exempt from the admissions tax.
Measure “H” does not alter the current exemption for admission charges paid
by “event sponsors” under Measure H, and that exemption from the tax would
remain in effect.
Measure “H” makes other clarifying amendments to the application process, such
as requiring organizations seeking an admissions tax exemption to demonstrate
to the City’s Director of Finance that they are in good standing.
Measure H requires any amendments to be approved by a majority of Indian
Wells voters and that is why Measure “H” is on the November 3, 2026, General
Municipal Election ballot.
A “yes” vote on Measure “ H “ will approve the amended exemptions identiﬁed
above.
A “no” vote on Measure “ H” will not approve the amended exemptions identiﬁed
above.
The above statement is an impartial analysis of Measure “H”. If you desire a
copy of the Measure, please call Angelica Avila, the City’s elections ofﬁcial
at (760) 346-2489 and a copy will be mailed at no cost to you.
